<Layout
  title="jeter-hermes Privacy Policy"
  description="How the jeter-hermes app uses Google data."
>
  <h2>jeter-hermes Privacy Policy</h2>
  <p><em>Effective November 18, 2026</em></p>

  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    jeter-hermes is a private, single-user app. It connects one person's Google account to a
    personal assistant system that runs on that person's own computer. It is not offered to the
    public, and it has no other users, no accounts and no sign-up.
  </p>
  <p>
    The app owner is also its only user. The owner runs it to sort their own email and manage
    their own calendar. This policy explains what Google data the app reads, what it does with
    it, and where it goes.
  </p>

  <h2>Google data the app accesses</h2>
  <p>The app requests three Google permissions (scopes), and uses each only for the purpose below.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>gmail.modify</code></strong>: reads messages to sort them; adds and removes
      labels; archives messages or returns them to the inbox; saves attachments for filing. It
      never deletes or trashes mail. Sending, replying, unsubscribing and marking spam happen only
      after the owner approves each one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>calendar.events</code></strong>: reads events to answer questions and avoid
      duplicates; adds events the owner has approved. Events that invite other people need the
      owner's separate approval code.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>calendar.calendarlist.readonly</code></strong>: lists the owner's calendars so
      events go to the right one. Read only.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>The app does not access Google Drive, Contacts, or any other Google service.</p>

  <h2>How data is processed and stored</h2>
  <p>
    Email is processed on the owner's own computer. Messages are read and sorted by an AI model
    that also runs on that computer, so email content is not sent to an outside AI service.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Stored locally:</strong> the Google sign-in token, a list of sender addresses and
      names, and short-lived working files about recent mail checks. They sit in the owner's home
      folder on the owner's computer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sent to the owner's private Discord server:</strong> short notices for the owner,
      such as a sender name and subject line for mail that needs attention, a count of what was
      archived, or a proposed list of calendar events. The server has no members besides the owner
      and the app's bots.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sent to outside AI services:</strong> the owner's assistant runs on OpenAI and can
      read those Discord notices when the owner asks it something. Photos of a family calendar or
      meal planner are read by OpenAI, or by Anthropic as a backup, to find events. Neither service
      receives the Gmail mailbox itself.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Attachments the owner chooses to keep are filed in the owner's private Nextcloud storage.</p>

  <h2>Sharing and Limited Use</h2>
  <p>
    Google data is never sold, rented, or used for advertising. It is shared only with the
    services named above, and only to provide features to the owner.
  </p>
  <p>
    jeter-hermes's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
    including the Limited Use requirements. In particular:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Google data is used only to provide the email and calendar features described here.</li>
    <li>It is transferred to others only as needed for those features, for security, or to comply with the law.</li>
    <li>No person reads it except the owner.</li>
    <li>It is not used to develop, improve, or train generalized AI or machine-learning models.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Retention and revoking access</h2>
  <p>The app keeps only what it needs to run; the mail and events themselves stay in Google.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Working files about mail checks are replaced on each run. The sender list is rebuilt from the last 12 months of mail.</li>
    <li>Discord notices stay in the owner's private server until the owner deletes them.</li>
    <li>
      To revoke access, remove jeter-hermes at
      <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections">Google Account → Third-party connections</a>.
      Deleting the token file on the owner's computer also ends access.
    </li>
    <li>Once access is revoked, the app stores no new Google data. Its local files can be deleted at any time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Security, changes and contact</h2>
  <p>
    The sign-in token and other secrets are stored only on the owner's computer, readable only by
    the owner's user account, and never kept in a code repository. Mail is never deleted by the
    app, and actions that send or reach other people need the owner's approval.
  </p>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the effective date above will be updated. Questions about it can go to
    the support email shown on the app's Google sign-in screen.
  </p>
</Layout>
